-# PS5 Relapse Exploit
+# PS5 Relapse · pldmgr edition (direct socket injection)
 Supported firmware: 7.00 through 13.60.
 
+This is a modified build of the Relapse exploit page whose end goal is
+**pldmgr v0.6.3 (itsPLK's Payload Manager) running as its own process**, with
+**everything cached on the console**:
+
+1. The moment the page opens - before the exploit even runs - **elfldr AND
+   pldmgr (plus the kexp shellcode) are saved to PS5 localStorage**, so repeat
+   runs need zero network for data. A service worker additionally keeps the
+   whole site inside the PS5 webkit cache.
+2. When the exploit runs, the **kexp shellcode is mapped first**, then the
+   kernel loader starts **elfldr** (the port-9021 ps5-payload-dev loader
+   daemon) - the only payload it can start reliably.
+3. **pldmgr is injected straight into the ELF loader over a raw socket**
+   (`src/inject.js`): after the kernel exploit the browser process is root and
+   sandbox-escaped, so the page does exactly what the documented
+   `nc -q0 <ps5> 9021 < payload.elf` does - `socket` → `connect 127.0.0.1:9021`
+   → stream the raw ELF → `shutdown` - all through ROP syscalls, **never**
+   through the browser network stack. elfldr sniffs the `\x7fELF` magic,
+   reads the payload by its own structure and spawns it with the connection
+   as its stdout. Success is then **proven, not guessed**: the page connects
+   to pldmgr's dashboard port **8084** and only reports success once it
+   answers, and pldmgr's first stdout lines are mirrored into the on-screen
+   log. pldmgr runs as its own process and serves
+   `http://<ps5-ip>:8084`.
+
+## Why the old browser-HTTP hand-over is gone
+The previous build asked elfldr to download the payload itself via
+`GET /?uri=<payload url>` (its mini HTTP server). That had two fatal flaws:
+elfldr answers those GETs with **valid `HTTP/1.1 200 OK` headers
+immediately**, so the old "the connection must stay pending" logic reported
+every delivery as an error and re-sent it for a minute; and on https pages
+(GitHub Pages, web portals) webkit blocks the no-cors fetch to
+`http://127.0.0.1` as active mixed content before any byte reaches the
+loader. The raw-socket injection has no browser-HTTP involvement at all, so
+none of that applies - it works the same on http and https hosting.
+
+## Why pldmgr is not started by the kernel loader
+The Relapse kernel loader is minimal: it starts elfldr (3 basic SPRX deps:
+libkernel_web, libSceLibcInternal, libSceNet) fine. pldmgr needs **nine**
+SPRX (adds libSceNetCtl, libSceUserService, libSceSystemService,
+libSceAppInstUtil, libSceHttp2, libSceSsl). Loading it directly hangs in
+kernel mode - the loader thread never returns, the browser process becomes
+unkillable, and the console browser then splash-loops on every launch until
+reboot. This is the "only elfldr runs / browser won't open anymore" glitch.
+The fix: never hand pldmgr to the kernel loader; deliver it through the full
+ps5-payload-dev ELF loader instead (it resolves SPRX via
+`sys_dynlib_process_needed_and_relocate` and spawns payloads as real
+processes). If your console is already stuck in the splash loop: **reboot
+once** - nothing is written to disk, the state clears on reboot.
+
+## What changed vs upstream
+- `src/kexp.js`: loads the **kexp shellcode first** (resolve → patch → map),
+  then elfldr, then runs the kernel loader **once**. pldmgr is never mapped
+  here (the 2.8 MB mapping was removed entirely - fewer primitive calls,
+  no second kernel session).
+- `src/payload_cache.js` (new): cache-first payload resolver - PS5 localStorage
+  → embedded base64 (`src/payload_data.js`) → network with write-back.
+- `src/payload_data.js`: elfldr (397 KB), pldmgr (2.8 MB) and the kexp
+  shellcode embedded as base64 with sha256 metadata; regenerated by
+  `scripts/build_payload_js.py`.
+- `src/site.js`: mirrors all three payloads into PS5 storage the moment the
+  page opens; registers the service worker; offsets are cached with a 7-day
+  refresh window and stale-cache fallback.
+- `sw.js` (new): service worker - page + offsets are network-first (updates
+  win when online), all other assets cache-first, so the site survives with
+  no network at all once it has been opened once. Bump `VERSION` in `sw.js`
+  whenever you change site files, so consoles pick up the new cache.
+- `src/inject.js` (new): the pldmgr delivery - raw-socket ELF injection into
+  port 9021 via ROP syscalls (socket/connect/write/shutdown/fcntl, all
+  present in every supported firmware's syscall table), with partial-write
+  handling, a port-8084 liveness probe (skips the injection entirely if
+  pldmgr is already running), a non-blocking stdout drain that mirrors
+  pldmgr's first log lines on screen, and two fallbacks if the loader never
+  answers: a browser-HTTP beacon plus a tap-to-launch `?uri=` link. A
+  Node end-to-end test (`scripts/test_inject.mjs`) drives the real module
+  over real TCP sockets against a faithful re-implementation of
+  socksrv.c/elfldr_read and asserts the loader receives a byte-identical
+  pldmgr ELF.
+- `index.html` / `serve.py`: cache-friendly headers, `?v=` cache busters on
+  script tags, short cache for offsets (they update often).
+
 ## Usage
-The default payloads are stored in `payloads/` after a successful run, the ELF loader listens on port `9021`.
+Open the page in the PS5 browser (or via the Jailbreak Store tile if you point
+`NEW_DEEPLINK` at your hosted copy). Everything runs automatically; the
+on-screen log shows each stage:
+
+```
+[+] kexp v2026-05-25 shellcode mapped at 0x...
+[+] elfldr v13.60 is up on port 9021
+[+] handing pldmgr v0.6.3 to the ELF loader over 127.0.0.1:9021 (direct socket)
+[+] pldmgr bytes streamed to the ELF loader (2795992 bytes)
+[+] pldmgr: pldmgr v0.6.3 booted        <- pldmgr's own stdout, mirrored live
+[+] pldmgr v0.6.3 is running as its own process (dashboard on port 8084)
+[+] you can close the browser - pldmgr keeps running
+```
+
+A PS5 notification with pldmgr's dashboard address (`http://<ps5-ip>:8084`)
+pops up once it is running. **Keep the page open until the "running as its
+own process" line appears** - the injection runs from the page. Unlike the
+old hand-over this needs **no internet on the PS5 at all**: the payload
+bytes come from the page's own cache (localStorage/embedded base64).
+
+## Notes
+- The pldmgr ELF is a standard ps5-payload-sdk binary (ET_DYN, 9 DT_NEEDED
+  SPRX deps) - it is spawned by the full ELF loader on port 9021, which
+  resolves those modules and runs pldmgr as its own process.
+- If the PS5 storage quota is exhausted (needs ~4.4 MB free), the payloads
+  simply use the embedded copy; nothing breaks.
+- First run needs the network once; after that the page, offsets and payloads
+  all come from the console (webkit cache + localStorage) - including the
+  pldmgr bytes that get injected into the loader.
+
+## Deploying to GitHub Pages
+1. Replace the repo content with this build (all files, including `sw.js` and
+   `src/payload_data.js`).
+2. Commit + push, wait for the Pages build (~1-2 min).
+3. On the PS5, open the URL once **online** - the page caches itself from then
+   on. If an old version was cached, append `?v=2` to the URL for the first
+   visit, or just reload twice (GitHub Pages sends a 10-minute cache TTL).
 
 ## Stability notes
 Webkit may need several attempts, reload the page if the browser stalls. The kernel exploit may hang or panic the console, so reboot before trying again if that happens.
